<?xml version="1.0"?>
<clickhouse>
  <!--
    Docker mem_limit for clickhouse service = 3072m (3 GiB), cpus = "1.0".
    Internal cap set to 2.5 GiB (2684354560 bytes) to leave ~512 MiB headroom
    for OS page cache and Docker overhead.

    History:
      Originally 800 MB - too low once system logs accumulated.
      Raised 2026-04-29 to 3.5 GiB cap when docker limit was 4096m.
      Reduced 2026-07-05: docker limit changed to 1536m (cap/comment were stale).
      Root cause of OOM death spiral: 3002+ fragmented parts in task_events table
      required > 1.5 GiB RSS at startup; container was OOM-killed 2344 times.
      Fix: raised docker limit to 3072m, cap to 2.5 GiB; truncated system logs;
      background merge will reduce part count over 2-3 days.

    DO NOT RAISE THIS VALUE. Four of the five ClickHouse incidents on this host
    were "fixed" by raising a ceiling; each time the bottleneck moved (RAM -> RAM
    -> RAM -> CPU) and the symptom looked new. The cap is a budget, and everything
    below is written to fit inside it. If something does not fit, shrink the thing,
    not the budget. The arithmetic is in the MERGE MEMORY BUDGET section.
  -->
  <max_server_memory_usage>2684354560</max_server_memory_usage>

  <!--
    REMOVED (was here until 2026-07-05, absence confirmed deliberate 2026-08-15):
      <profiles><default><allow_experimental_json_type>1</allow_experimental_json_type>

    It existed because v4 Goose migrations (task_runs_v1 etc.) use JSON columns and
    ClickHouse 24.x gated that type behind an experimental flag. On the pinned
    25.7.x line the setting reports value=1, changed=0, tier=Production - i.e. it is
    the built-in default and no longer experimental. Verified on 25.7.5 by creating a
    JSON column successfully and by the fact that trigger_dev.task_runs_v2 /
    task_events_v2 / metrics_v1 already store JSON columns and work.

    Do not re-add it: it is dead configuration on the pinned version. If ClickHouse is
    ever downgraded below 25.x (AGENTS.md forbids this), restore it first.
  -->

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       2026-08-25 - MERGE MEMORY BUDGET (incident #5 root cause)

       Symptom: container pegged at 100% CPU for three days; system.error_log
       recorded 29,507 MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED (code 241) in six hours; three
       concurrent merges on system.metric_log sat at progress=0 forever:

         (total) memory limit exceeded: would use 2.50 GiB
         (attempt to allocate chunk of 4.16 MiB bytes),
         current RSS: 1.18 GiB, maximum: 2.50 GiB

       Mechanism - this is a WIDTH problem, not a volume problem:
         system.metric_log       1,344 columns
         system.query_metric_log   939 columns
         system.query_log           84 columns   <- everything else is narrow
       A merge opens one write stream per column, each with its own compression
       buffer, so a metric_log merge costs ~800 MiB no matter how few rows it
       merges. That cost cannot be tuned away; it follows the column count.
       Vertical merge does not rescue it either: the table was already above
       vertical_merge_algorithm_min_rows_to_activate (131072) and still cost that.

       Why it became a permanent loop rather than a slow query:
         merge needs ~800 MiB -> total would exceed 2.5 GiB -> merge aborts ->
         parts are not consumed (83 active parts) -> next attempt picks up even
         MORE parts -> aborts again -> each abort writes a line to system.text_log
         -> text_log became the largest system table (171 MiB, 116,770 error rows
         in 24h) -> which is itself what pushed the system/app ratio to 8.3x.
       The alarm was partly measuring the alarm's own error spam. Nothing in that
       loop self-corrects, and it survives restarts: it is a ratchet.

       THE DEFECT, stated precisely (two layers - the second is the one that
       makes this a design fault rather than a missing setting):

         1. Background merges had no memory bound at all.
            merges_mutations_memory_usage_soft_limit reported 0 at runtime, and
            that setting's own documentation says 0 means unlimited. So the
            scheduler was free to start a second and third ~800 MiB merge while
            the first was still running, and 3 x ~800 MiB plus base RSS is over
            cap. (system.server_settings reports EFFECTIVE values, not file
            values - proven on the disposable container used to validate this
            file, where a configured 2.5 GiB max_server_memory_usage was read
            back as 1.05 GiB after ClickHouse clamped it to that container's
            1200m cgroup. The 0 was the real runtime state, not an unread line.)

         2. Setting that bound does not fix it, which is the important part.
            With the limit set to 1 GiB, ClickHouse still scheduled TWO
            concurrent metric_log merges - measured on production, not assumed.
            The scheduler estimates a merge's memory from the parts' size on
            disk (~115 MiB for the whole table) and is blind to the per-column
            write buffers that make the real cost ~800 MiB. A budget cannot be
            enforced by an estimator that cannot see the cost.

       So the defect is not "a setting was missing". It is that a table existed
       whose merge cost neither fits the budget NOR is visible to the mechanism
       that is supposed to police the budget. Only removing such a table closes
       it; the soft limit below is kept as defence in depth for future tables
       whose cost the estimator CAN see.

       THE INVARIANT:
         no system-log table may be wide enough that one merge of it exceeds the
         merge budget - i.e. base RSS (~0.9 GiB) + concurrent merges <= 2.5 GiB.

       Two knobs were tried against this invariant and BOTH were rejected on
       measurement, which is worth recording so nobody re-proposes them:

         background_pool_size=2 - refused to start. The pool size is load-bearing
           for three other settings (number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_
           mutation = 20, ..._to_lower_max_size_of_merge = 8, ..._to_execute_
           optimize_entire_partition = 25) and ClickHouse exits with code 36
           BAD_ARGUMENTS when the pool is smaller than the first. Verified on a
           disposable container. Sizing it down consistently is possible, but a
           pool small enough to serialise wide merges also serialises application
           merges - which is what produced 2026-07-05 (3002 fragmented parts).

         merges_mutations_memory_usage_soft_limit=1GiB - applied cleanly but did
           not bind, for the reason given in layer 2 of THE DEFECT above. Kept
           regardless, as defence in depth for tables the estimator CAN size.

       So the invariant is met the only way it can be: by removing the two tables
       whose merges do not fit.

       Fixes applied, in order of how much they matter:
         1. metric_log removed        - 1,344 columns, the incident-#5 cause
         2. query_metric_log removed  - 939 columns, the same hazard, dormant
         3. background merges bounded by memory (immediately below)
       After this file the widest system log table is query_log at 84 columns.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- Hard budget for all background merges and mutations combined. Must stay
       comfortably below max_server_memory_usage minus steady-state RSS. This is
       the single setting that makes the incident-#5 loop impossible: when merges
       already hold 1 GiB, the scheduler starts no more of them, so the total can
       no longer reach the cap and abort the merge that was already running.
       If ClickHouse ever grows metric_log past ~1 GiB per merge, this bound must
       be re-derived - check-clickhouse-health.ps1 alerts on merge failures. -->
  <merges_mutations_memory_usage_soft_limit>1073741824</merges_mutations_memory_usage_soft_limit>

  <!-- ── System log generators ────────────────────────────────────────────
       Ordering note: retention (TTL) bounds how long rows are KEPT; it does
       nothing about how fast they are PRODUCED, and merge cost follows
       production. Every generator below is therefore configured at the source
       first and given a TTL second.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->

  <!-- trace_log: the sampling profiler. 10.25 GiB / 446M rows, 1.24M rows/hour,
       and never queried by anyone here. Removing the config disables the trace
       collector outright, which is the documented way to switch it off. Re-enable
       temporarily (delete this line, restart) only for a deliberate perf
       investigation, and turn it back off afterwards. -->
  <trace_log remove="1"/>

  <!-- asynchronous_metric_log: 99.5M rows, and its event_date range started at
       1970-01-01, so part pruning could not work on it either. Host-level metrics
       are already collected by Netdata + Grafana, so this table is pure overhead. -->
  <asynchronous_metric_log remove="1"/>

  <!-- query_metric_log: 939 columns - the second-widest table in the instance and
       therefore the second source of ~800 MiB merges. It currently holds 1 row, so
       it looks harmless, but it fills per-query once queries run long enough to be
       sampled, and at that point it is indistinguishable from the metric_log
       problem above. system.query_log (84 columns) already records per-query
       timing, memory and ProfileEvents, which is what anyone here would read.
       Removing it makes "at most one wide table" a property of the config rather
       than a coincidence of current traffic - that is what turns the budget above
       into an invariant instead of a lucky measurement. -->
  <query_metric_log remove="1"/>

  <!-- metric_log: REMOVED. 1,344 columns - the table whose merges cost ~800 MiB
       each and which caused incident #5. It was deliberately kept on 2026-08-15
       as "the only ClickHouse-internal metric source", with only its flush
       interval slowed. That judgement is reversed here, and the reason is not
       "it got worse" - it is that keeping it makes the memory budget unenforceable
       in principle:

         A single background pool holds both this table's ~800 MiB merges and
         trigger_dev's ~5 MiB merges, with ~1.3 GiB of spare budget. Sizing the
         pool so that only ONE wide merge can run (background_pool_size=1) also
         serialises every application merge - which is how 2026-07-05 happened
         (3002 fragmented parts in task_events, 2344 OOM-kills). Sizing it for
         application throughput lets two wide merges overlap and blow the cap.
         The two requirements are in direct conflict FOR AS LONG AS a table this
         wide shares the pool. No setting resolves that; removing the table does.

       And the memory limit below does not rescue it either, which was measured
       rather than assumed: with merges_mutations_memory_usage_soft_limit set to
       1 GiB, ClickHouse still scheduled TWO concurrent metric_log merges. Its
       scheduler estimates merge cost from part size on disk (~115 MiB total),
       and is blind to the per-column write buffers that make the real cost ~800
       MiB. An estimator that cannot see the cost cannot enforce a budget on it.

       What is lost: a time series of ClickHouse-internal counters that nothing
       here has ever queried. What remains: system.metrics / system.events (live
       counters), system.query_log (84 columns, per-query ProfileEvents and
       memory), system.error_log, system.text_log, system.part_log,
       system.processors_profile_log, plus Netdata + Grafana at host and
       container level. asynchronous_metric_log was removed on 2026-08-15 for
       the same reason and nothing was lost.

       THE RULE THIS ESTABLISHES: no system-log table may be wide enough that a
       single merge of it exceeds the merge budget. After this file, the widest
       remaining system log table is query_log at 84 columns.
       check-clickhouse-health.ps1 alerts if a wide one ever reappears. -->
  <metric_log remove="1"/>

  <!-- text_log: 5.33 GiB / 90M rows in 14 days = ~6.4M rows/day, because it
       mirrors EVERY server log line into a table. Container logs already go to
       Docker's json driver, so this exists only for in-SQL post-incident search.
       Restricting it to warning-and-above keeps that value and removes the flood;
       3 days is enough for the incident window it is actually used in.
       2026-08-25: this table reaching 171 MiB was a SYMPTOM of the merge loop
       above (116,770 merge-failure lines in 24h), not a generator problem of its
       own. No change needed here; it shrinks on its own once merges succeed. -->
  <text_log>
    <level>warning</level>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    <flush_interval_milliseconds>30000</flush_interval_milliseconds>
  </text_log>

  <!-- query_thread_log / s3queue_log: REMOVED. Both exist on disk here as empty
       tables, and neither has a writer in this stack: query_thread_log only fills
       when log_query_threads is enabled (per-THREAD rows, i.e. a multiple of
       query_log's volume), and s3queue_log only when an S3Queue table engine is
       used. Leaving an unwritten table with no TTL sitting there is the exact
       shape of the 2026-08-19 part_log finding, just with the clock not started
       yet. Removing them means the clock can never start. -->
  <query_thread_log remove="1"/>
  <s3queue_log remove="1"/>

  <!-- ── System log TTL: tiered retention policy ──────────────────────────
       Rationale:
         3d  - high-churn text/insert logs
         7d  - part & span logs (replaced by Netdata for long-term trends)
         14d - query & view logs (useful for post-incident perf regression)
         30d - error log (recurring / rare bugs need a month of history)
         90d - crash & backup logs (rare, small, and the most valuable to still
               have when someone finally goes looking)

       THE RULE: every MergeTree table in the `system` database must carry a TTL.
       It does not matter whether the TTL comes from this file or from ClickHouse's
       own base config (blob_storage_log, for instance, ships with one) - what
       matters is the outcome on the running server, because that is the thing that
       actually bounds disk growth. check-clickhouse-health.ps1 checks the outcome
       in system.tables on every AO-RESUME, so a table that ClickHouse adds in a
       future version with no TTL is caught the same way part_log was.

       A TTL applied with a live ALTER does NOT satisfy this rule: it is erased by
       the next restart. See the 2026-08-25 section at the end of this file.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <processors_profile_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </processors_profile_log>
  <latency_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </latency_log>
  <query_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </query_log>
  <error_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 30 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </error_log>
  <asynchronous_insert_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </asynchronous_insert_log>
  <!-- Rare and small, but the two you most regret not having. Note crash_log has
       no partition_by in the base config; TTL still applies, it is just evaluated
       without partition pruning - harmless at this table's volume. -->
  <crash_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 90 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </crash_log>
  <backup_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 90 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </backup_log>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       2026-08-25 - part_log / query_views_log / opentelemetry_span_log:
       TTL moved from live ALTER into this file (the 2026-08-19 gap, closed)

       What 2026-08-19 concluded: "these four tables reject the declarative
       <ttl> form, reason unconfirmed", so their TTL was applied with live
       `ALTER TABLE ... MODIFY TTL` and the health check was added as a
       compensating control. That conclusion was WRONG, and the way it was
       wrong is worth keeping: all four were declared in one edit, ONE of them
       crashed the container, and the whole batch was blamed for it.

       What is actually true - read from the running instance's MERGED config at
       /bitnami/clickhouse/data/preprocessed_configs/config.xml, which is the
       file 2026-08-19 did not look at (it checked the base config.xml only):

         opentelemetry_span_log  base config DOES define <engine>, with an
                                 upstream comment calling it a workaround for
                                 this table having no 'event_time'. A sibling
                                 <ttl> is therefore rejected exactly as the
                                 error said: "If 'engine' is specified for
                                 system table, TTL parameters should be
                                 specified directly inside 'engine'".
         part_log                no <engine>. Same shape as
         query_views_log         processors_profile_log, which has carried a
         query_metric_log        declarative <ttl> from this file for months.

       So: three take the plain form, one needs the TTL inside <engine>, and
       query_metric_log is removed above, so it needs neither.

       Why this matters more than the TTLs themselves: a live ALTER is not
       durable. The EU host auto-reboots at 20:30 UTC (runbooks/
       VPS-AUTO-REBOOT-RUNBOOK.md); on the 2026-08-22 reboot ClickHouse rebuilt
       these three tables from config, renamed the ALTER'd ones to <table>_0,
       and the new ones came up with no TTL. Verified after the fact: the _0
       orphans still carried the TTL, the live tables did not. Auto-reboot and
       ClickHouse were each behaving correctly on their own; the defect was a
       correction stored outside the only place that survives a restart. The
       config-declared TTLs came through that same reboot untouched, which is
       the entire argument for this section.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <part_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </part_log>
  <query_views_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </query_views_log>

  <!-- TTL embedded in <engine> because the base config defines an engine for this
       table (see above). Partition and order keys are copied verbatim from the
       upstream definition - changing them is not the intent, adding TTL is. -->
  <opentelemetry_span_log>
    <engine>
      engine MergeTree
      partition by toYYYYMM(finish_date)
      order by (finish_date, finish_time_us)
      ttl finish_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE
    </engine>
  </opentelemetry_span_log>
</clickhouse>
